6. STRICT RULE E — UNIVERSAL ENERGY COST IS 15

Every move or action that consumes Energy must now cost exactly the same nominal amount: 15 Energy.

No fighter may have a unique Energy cost.

No move may have a special Energy price such as 20, 25, 30, or 40.

The current Dash cost of 20 must become 15.

Universal costs

Action	Energy cost
Ground Dash	15
Air Dash	15
Dash cancel	15
Deflect	15
Shield-blocked hit	15
Perfect Shield-blocked hit	15
Eligible ordinary projectile move	15
Eligible ordinary trap deployment	15
Eligible ordinary summon	15
Eligible ordinary clone	15
Eligible ordinary gameplay object	15
Eligible ordinary field/burst or similar spawned gameplay effect	15

Holding Shield should remain free; blocking a hit consumes Energy.

Normal running, jumping, turning, and ordinary melee attacks that do not create cost-bearing gameplay entities should remain free unless another existing mechanic explicitly requires Energy.

Do not make every attack consume Energy.

The new Energy requirement for generated gameplay entities is governed by the next rule.

New Energy costs for projectiles, traps, summons, clones, and objects

All player-generated or CPU-generated offensive and utility gameplay constructs whose parent move does NOT have a 3-second or 5-second cooldown must consume 15 Energy.

This includes all applicable:

* Projectiles.
* Thrown objects.
* Summoned clones.
* Summoned entities.
* Deployed traps.
* Persistent damaging areas.
* Autonomous objects.
* Spawned gameplay constructs.
* Gameplay-affecting bursts or fields.
* Future additions using equivalent mechanics.

The exact name of the move does not matter.

The rule must be determined from what the move actually creates and from its cooldown classification.

Use structured move data, not a hardcoded list of current character names.

A construct-producing move with a 3s or 5s cooldown should not receive this additional 15-Energy requirement.

Its cooldown is already the restriction assigned by this rule set.

For example:

* An ordinary projectile that previously had the 0.5s repeat cooldown now has no cooldown, but costs 15 Energy.
* A normal clone deployment without a 3s/5s cooldown costs 15 Energy.
* A projectile technique with an exceptional 3s cooldown keeps its 3s cooldown and does not gain the new construct Energy charge.
* A 5s ultimate projectile keeps its 5s cooldown without an additional construct charge.

One activation, one charge

A single move activation should consume its Energy cost only once.

Do not charge 15 for every individual hit from the same projectile.

Do not charge 15 repeatedly for:

* Each projectile update.
* Each frame a trap remains alive.
* Each hit from a clone.
* Each clone animation.
* A projectile piercing multiple opponents.
* A projectile being reflected.
* A persistent area’s repeated collision checks.
* Multiple phases of one already-paid attack.

If one move creates several components as part of one authored activation, treat them as one parent activation for the Energy cost unless the data defines separate independently activated moves.

Validate the move’s actual eligibility before charging Energy. Rejected inputs must not consume it.

Keep Energy accounting deterministic, including interrupted startup animations and reused moves.

Do not invent Energy refunds for already committed actions unless needed to correct a duplicate or invalid charge.

Preserve the existing Energy model unless explicitly changed

Retain the existing:

* 100 maximum Energy.
* Per-fighter passive regeneration rates.
* Exhaustion state.
* Exhaustion recovery requirements.
* Shield mechanics.
* Zero-energy restrictions.
* Reset and respawn behavior.

Alva currently allows some Energy actions to drain the remaining bar even when it contains less than the nominal cost, causing exhaustion until fully replenished.

Preserve that underlying partial-spend behavior unless it would cause a violation of the new 15-Energy activation rules. Do not silently introduce a completely different Energy regeneration or exhaustion system.

Importantly, all newly Energy-costing constructs must participate in the same exhaustion and resource eligibility checks as existing Energy actions.

No fighter should be able to use ordinary projectiles for free simply because the code previously regarded all attacks as Energy-free.

Shared source of truth

Prefer one shared Energy cost constant or centrally derived policy.

Avoid scattered literal 15 values in every character implementation.

Update resolveEnergy() and its schema validation so fighter-specific cost overrides cannot violate the universal rules.

