7. STRICT RULE F — CPU FIGHTERS MUST USE THE SAME SYSTEM

All changes must apply equally to human players and CPU-controlled fighters.

This is mandatory.

The CPU cannot use a separate old implementation of cooldowns, Energy, or cancellation.

Cancellation parity

The CPU must obey the same attack-cancellation restrictions as the player.

It must NOT freely interrupt damaging attacks outside legitimate cancel windows.

It must retain legitimate hit-confirmed combos and authored follow-ups.

However, CPU fighters must also be allowed to cancel non-attacking moves and Deflect under the same conditions as human fighters.

Do not keep the new cancellation rules exclusive to controller.kind === 'player'.

Implement the relevant permissions at a shared fighter/gameplay level.

The CPU should be able to intentionally cancel a utility move or Deflect when a better valid action becomes available.

It should not spam cancellation every frame or repeatedly select impossible replacement moves.

Cooldown awareness

The CPU must understand:

* Ordinary moves have no 0.5s cooldown.
* Exceptional moves have 3s cooldowns.
* Ten-damage moves have 5s cooldowns.
* A 3s/5s cooldown begins only after the move releases.
* An interrupted move that never released does not start its cooldown.
* A released move remains unavailable until its cooldown expires.
* A cooldown is not the same as an animation lock.
* Moves without cooldowns can still be unavailable because the fighter is busy or a different eligibility rule blocks them.

It must never plan a special move as ready while it is actually cooling down.

It must also avoid treating a move as permanently unavailable simply because an earlier startup animation was interrupted.

Energy awareness

The CPU must understand that normal projectiles and construct-producing moves now spend Energy.

Update CPU capabilities, move readiness, planning, and evaluation so that it correctly accounts for the 15-Energy cost.

The CPU must not:

* Fire projectiles without paying.
* Summon clones for free when their move requires Energy.
* Assume all attacks are Energy-free.
* Use an exhausted fighter’s resources illegally.
* Continue selecting unavailable cost-bearing moves.
* Pay an incorrect Dash cost.
* Use outdated cooldown or availability information.

Its decision-making should consider the actual Energy left after a move.

A fighter should still make intelligent tradeoffs among attacks, summons, defense, and mobility.

The CPU must not become completely passive or stop using projectiles because of the new costs.

