8. IMPORTANT INTERACTION RULES AND EDGE CASES

The new systems must coexist correctly.

Case A: Attack → different attack

If Attack 1 is an ongoing damaging attack and Attack 2 is requested before the first attack is cancellable:

Attack 1 continues.

Attack 2 may be buffered according to existing rules, but it must not forcibly replace Attack 1.

Case B: Attack → Dash

If the attack has not entered a legitimate cancellation window, Dash cannot cut it short merely because the player requested it.

If the attack has entered its valid hit-confirmed Dash cancellation window, the Dash can start according to the existing timing restrictions and costs 15 Energy.

Case C: Deflect → aerial attack

An airborne fighter starts Deflect and then requests a different valid aerial attack.

Deflect must be cancellable.

The old Deflect hitbox and reflection effect must stop when cancelled.

The new attack begins only after its eligibility checks succeed.

Case D: Deflect → air Dash

Deflect is interrupted by a valid air Dash.

Deflect has already consumed its 15 Energy.

The air Dash consumes its own 15 Energy.

Do not combine both actions into a single charge.

Case E: Ordinary projectile

A move with no special cooldown creates a projectile.

The move consumes 15 Energy exactly once, has no universal 0.5s cooldown, and remains governed by its animation and normal action restrictions.

Case F: Exceptional projectile interrupted during casting

A 3s projectile technique begins casting but is interrupted before release.

No projectile appears.

No 3s cooldown starts.

Any Energy behavior must follow the move’s actual established cost rules.

Case G: Exceptional projectile successfully released

The projectile actually appears.

The 3s cooldown starts immediately at that release event.

The projectile may hit, miss, or be reflected without changing the timer.

Case H: Summon interrupted before spawning

The fighter starts a summon whose release requires a startup animation.

The fighter is hit before the summon materializes.

The summon does not appear.

If it is a 3s/5s summon, that cooldown must not start.

If it is a cost-bearing ordinary summon, its Energy handling must match the defined one-time activation charge without duplicate deductions.

Case I: Summon successfully released

The clone materializes.

Its 3s/5s cooldown, if applicable, begins then.

Subsequent clone attacks do not restart the cooldown.

For an ordinary cost-bearing clone, the 15-Energy cost must not repeat when the clone attacks.

Case J: Healing technique

A non-attacking healing technique begins casting.

The fighter is allowed to cancel it with a valid replacement action.

If cancelled before healing is released, no 3s cooldown begins.

If healing has already been executed, the 3s cooldown starts even if the fighter later leaves the recovery animation.

Case K: Zero cooldown, same attack

A fighter presses an ordinary melee attack repeatedly while that same attack animation is running.

The move must not continuously restart itself.

Once the attack finishes naturally, it can be used again, assuming every other rule allows it.

Case L: CPU Deflect cancellation

A CPU starts Deflect and recognizes an opportunity to use a different valid move.

It can cancel Deflect just as the player can.

It must pay the same Energy and satisfy the same eligibility requirements.

Case M: Interrupted multi-hit special

A multi-hit move that qualifies for the 5s cooldown is interrupted before its complete required sequence is released.

Do not start its 5s cooldown merely because the first animation or an early strike began.

If its complete authored attack sequence is released, the cooldown begins and cannot be bypassed by later interruption.

Case N: Multiple effects from one action

A technique releases a projectile and a burst, or an attack creates multiple connected gameplay effects.

Do not charge Energy separately for each component if they belong to one activation.

Do not start a cooldown separately for each hit or component.

The move’s complete authored release event should trigger its cooldown exactly once.

Case O: Reset, death, and match transitions

Do not leave pending cooldown events, ghost clones, stale release flags, or repeated Energy charges after:

* Death.
* Respawn.
* Match restart.
* Practice reset.
* Void transitions.
* Fighter destruction.
* CPU plan cancellation.
* Battle teardown.

Preserve existing behavior regarding active projectile persistence and cooldown clearing where those rules are not superseded.

